WORM                           OS-9/68000                           WORMNAME     worm -- play the growing worm gameSYNOPSIS     worm [size]DESCRIPTION     You are a little worm: your body is the `o's on the screen and your     head is the `@'.  You move with the h, j, k and l keys, as in snake.     If you press no key, you carry on the way you last went.  The capital     H, J, K and L move as though you had pressed the small key several     times over -- nine for H and L, five for J and K -- unless you run     into a digit, which stops you.     There is always a digit somewhere on the screen.  Eat it and the worm     grows longer by that many.  The object is to see how long you can make     the worm grow.  The game ends when the worm runs into the side of the     screen or into itself.  The score -- how much the worm has grown -- is     kept at the top of the screen.     The optional argument is the worm's starting length.  Control-C     leaves the game at any time.BUGS     A starting length under one or over about 75 does strange things.OS-9     The Unix worm keeps moving on a one-second alarm.  This version waits     for a key for up to a second instead, and moves on when none comes.     There is no job control, so ^Z does nothing.  The source, and the     original as released, are in SRC/worm.ORIGIN     worm.c 5.8 (Berkeley) 2/28/91 and its manual page, from 4.3BSD Net/2.     Written by Michael Toy at UCSC.     This product includes software developed by the University of     California, Berkeley and its contributors.     Copyright (c) 1980 Regents of the University of California.     All rights reserved.     Redistribution and use in source and binary forms, with or without     modification, are permitted provided that the following conditions     are met:     1. Redistributions of source code must retain the above copyright        notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer.     2. Redistribution